# A privileged process must not hand sensitive data to a less privileged one without
# first checking that the recipient is permitted to receive it. Under site isolation a
# web content process is entitled to the cookies of its own site and no others, so a
# cookie may only be sent to it once someone has proven that this process may have this
# cookie.
#
# The type system enforces this. A parameter declared as IPC::PermissionChecked<T> in a
# .messages.in file makes the generated message class constructible only from a token,
# so every sender must obtain one, and the only ways to obtain one are:
#
#   PermissionChecked<T>::check(checker, value)      - runs one of the pre-ordained
#                                                      permission checks, which confirm
#                                                      the recipient is entitled to the
#                                                      value
#   PermissionChecked<T>::withoutPermissionCheck(value, reason)
#                                                    - bypass, and must name a
#                                                      justification
#
# The wrapper has no decoder, so it cannot arrive over IPC: it can only be minted in the
# process that holds the authority to mint it. Recipients are unaffected; their handler
# signatures see a plain T.
#
# See Source/WebKit/Platform/IPC/PermissionChecked.h for the wrapper, and
# Source/WebKit/NetworkProcess/CookieRecipientAuthority.h for the permission check.
#
# This file lists the parameters that are NOT yet wrapped. Every entry is a code path
# where a privileged process hands sensitive data to a less privileged process without
# proving it is entitled to it. The build fails on any unlisted occurrence, so the list
# can only shrink.
#
# Additions or changes to this file will trigger a review with a security focus.

# Each entry must carry one of the following justifications (in square brackets):
#
# LegacyNeedsAudit:      Predates this mechanism and has not been audited. This is the
#                        burn-down list, not an assertion that the code path is safe.
# CheckedElsewhere:      An equivalent permission check already happens on this code path.
# NotSecuritySensitive:  The value is not sensitive in this context.
# NeedsReview:           Needs a security review before a final justification is given.
#
# MessageParam covers a parameter of a message, which travels in the direction the
# message is dispatched. MessageParamReply covers a parameter of a reply, which travels
# the opposite way.
#
# Format, either grouped by type:
#
#   [Justification] TypeString {
#       MessageParam Receiver.Message parameterName
#       MessageParamReply Receiver.Message parameterName
#   }
#
# or one entry per line:
#
#   [Justification] MessageParam Receiver.Message parameterName TypeString

# The web extension cookie APIs gate access with [Validator=isCookiesMessageAllowed] and
# host permission checks in the UI process, but whether those cover every path by which a
# content script can name another site's cookies has not been audited here.
[NeedsReview] Expected<std::optional<WebKit::WebExtensionCookieParameters>, WebKit::WebExtensionError> {
    MessageParamReply WebExtensionContext.CookiesGet result
    MessageParamReply WebExtensionContext.CookiesSet result
    MessageParamReply WebExtensionContext.CookiesRemove result
}

[NeedsReview] MessageParamReply WebExtensionContext.CookiesGetAll result Expected<Vector<WebKit::WebExtensionCookieParameters>, WebKit::WebExtensionError>
